# Data

`Data` is a fundamental HQL type for structured information.

It is not HQL's equivalent of a `struct`, record, object, or database row. Those abstractions describe values according to a predefined set of fields. `Data` instead describes a tree whose structure is carried by the value itself.

The closest existing representation is YAML. Conceptually, an HQL `Data` value should be able to represent a YAML document essentially one-to-one.

```yaml
name: Alice
age: 42

address:
  city: Zürich
  country: CH

roles:
  - admin
  - author
```

This is naturally a `Data` tree:

```text
Data
└── mapping
    ├── name
    │   └── "Alice"
    ├── age
    │   └── 42
    ├── address
    │   └── mapping
    │       ├── city
    │       │   └── "Zürich"
    │       └── country
    │           └── "CH"
    └── roles
        └── sequence
            ├── "admin"
            └── "author"
```

At the conceptual level, `Data` is recursive:

```text
Data =
    Scalar
  | Sequence<Data>
  | Mapping<String, Data>
```

The exact implementation does not have to use this definition literally. What matters is the model: `Data` is a tree made from mappings, sequences, and scalar values.

## Metadata is Data

One of the main purposes of `Data` is metadata.

Metadata usually does not deserve a new HQL record type merely because it has several named properties. Its natural representation is a `Data` tree.

A document header is the canonical example. Every [[doc-type|Doc]] — and so
every [[card-type|card]], which is a narrowed document — carries a `header` that
is a `Data` tree.

A card may begin with optional YAML metadata:

```yaml
---
title: Data
tags: [hql, type-system]
status: draft

source:
  format: yaml
  version: 1
---
```

The complete header is `Data`.

Conceptually:

```hql
doc.header : Data
```

`header` is the name, because a fenced YAML block is only a Markdown-specific way of writing the authored part of a header, while the rest of a header is derived by the host or contributed by an extension. The header is not optional: a document with no metadata block has an empty header. See [[doc-type]].

The metadata can be traversed naturally:

```hql
doc.header.title
doc.header.format
doc.header.tags
doc.header.status
```

A single `Data` tree is what lets derived entries such as `format` and `path` sit beside authored keys and extension-contributed ones without three separate metadata types.

These expressions do not imply that HQL contains a statically declared header struct with fields called `title`, `tags`, `status`, and `source`.

They are paths through a `Data` tree.

This distinction is central because a document header is deliberately extensible. HMD reserves only `tags`, `use`, `import`, and `nav` in the authored block; every other key belongs to the author, and the derived and contributed layers add keys no author wrote. Different cards, applications, extensions, and namespaces can place different metadata there without extending the HQL type system every time a new YAML key appears.

The same principle applies beyond document headers. Configuration, annotations, extension metadata, query metadata, imported YAML, JSON-like API responses, database JSON values, and similar structured payloads are naturally `Data`.

In other words, when HQL encounters structured metadata for which there is no stronger semantic type, the default type is `Data`.

## Data is not a record

A record describes a known shape.

For example, HQL may eventually support a type such as:

```hql
type Person = {
    name: String
    age: Int
}
```

A value of `Person` is constrained by the definition of `Person`.

`Data` has no such requirement.

```yaml
name: Alice
age: 42
```

and:

```yaml
server:
  host: localhost
  ports:
    - 8080
    - 8443
```

are both perfectly valid `Data` values even though their shapes have nothing in common.

A record therefore answers:

> What is the declared shape of this value?

`Data` answers:

> What tree is present here?

This also distinguishes `Data` from a database row. A row belongs to a relation with columns and a schema. `Data` is hierarchical and carries arbitrary nesting.

## Structure first, stronger types later

Representing something as `Data` does not prevent HQL from giving parts of it stronger types.

A tree can be interpreted, validated, or converted later.

Conceptually:

```hql
metadata : Data = ...

person : Person =
    metadata.as<Person>()
```

This gives HQL a useful separation between raw structured information and typed interpretation. Interpretation need not copy: HMD, extensions and the program itself progressively establish stronger types for regions of one unchanged tree. See [[type-system]].

The source can remain faithful to YAML while an extension, function, schema, or application imposes additional meaning where necessary.

The same idea applies to HyperMarkDown itself. The entire header remains `Data`, while HMD or HQL can assign special meaning to particular entries.

The tree does not have to stop being `Data` merely because some of its paths are understood by the system.

This is what makes card types narrow rather than being declared up front. See [[card-type]].

## YAML as the native serialization of Data

YAML provides a useful way of thinking about the external representation of `Data`.

Loading YAML should naturally produce `Data`:

```hql
config : Data = yaml.load("config.yaml")
```

and a `Data` value should naturally be serializable back to YAML:

```hql
yaml.dump(config)
```

This relationship should be sufficiently direct that HQL does not need an intermediate generic object model merely to represent a YAML document.

JSON can fit the same tree model, although YAML is the more direct model for HyperMarkDown because HMD already uses YAML for its optional authored block.

This makes `Data` the common structural substrate between HQL, HyperMarkDown metadata, configuration, and external structured information.

## Paths

Because `Data` is a tree, path navigation is one of its fundamental operations.

Given:

```yaml
database:
  primary:
    host: db.example.org
    port: 5432
```

the natural HQL expression is:

```hql
data.database.primary.host
```

Dynamic access can use the same tree without pretending the keys were statically declared fields:

```hql
data["database"]["primary"]["host"]
```

The precise path API remains part of the HQL language design, but path traversal follows directly from the nature of `Data`.

## Data and HQL extensions

`Data` is also an important boundary type for the HQL extension architecture.

An extension frequently needs to expose structured information without introducing an entire hierarchy of new HQL types.

For example:

```hql
postgres.describe("users") : Data
```

Extension-contributed card metadata works the same way, which is why extensions can enrich a card without the HQL core gaining a document type per extension. See [[hql/extensions]].



or:

```hql
git.metadata(...) : Data
```

or:

```hql
http.get(...).yaml() : Data
```

The extension can preserve the source structure as `Data`. Stronger HQL types can be added where there is a genuine semantic reason for them.

This keeps the HQL core type system small while still allowing extensions to expose arbitrarily rich structured information.

## Data and knowledge

`Data` describes structure, not meaning.

For example:

```yaml
alice:
  daughter: bob
```

is a `Data` tree.

Its presence alone does not necessarily establish a semantic HQL relation such as:

```text
daughterOf(Alice, Bob)
```

That requires interpretation.

This creates an important boundary in HQL:

```text
Data       structured information
Knowledge  semantic information
```

`Data` can contain a representation of knowledge, just as YAML can contain a representation of almost anything. But the tree itself is not automatically the semantic model.

A parser, schema, declaration, or extension can interpret `Data` and produce concepts, relations, propositions, evidence, or other HQL values.

## Data as a central HQL type

`Data` should therefore be treated as one of HQL's core types rather than as a convenience container.

It is the native type of arbitrary hierarchical structured information.

A HyperMarkDown header is `Data`.

YAML documents are `Data`.

Most untyped or loosely typed metadata is `Data`.

Extension-defined structured metadata can be `Data`.

Configuration and external JSON/YAML-like payloads can be `Data`.

The defining idea is not that `Data` is a generic object or `Map<String, Any>`.

The defining idea is:

```text
Data is a structured value whose shape is a tree.
```

That tree can later be queried, transformed, validated, interpreted, or converted into stronger HQL types without losing the original structure.

See [[doc-type]] for the document header, [[card-type]] for the knowledge-bearing
kind of document, [[pipes]] for composition, and [[hql/knowledge]] for the
semantic side of the `Data`/`Knowledge` boundary.
